Ladybird routes an extension’s runtime messages in the UI process:
every page registers the contexts it hosts, a message goes to each
context of the extension’s own pages but the sender’s, the first reply
settles the request, and declines from every target mean nobody
replied. WebKit’s WebExtensionContext::runtimeSendMessage() picks the
processes with a listener for the event through processes(eventType,
contentWorldType), sends each a DispatchRuntimeMessageEvent with an
async reply, and an EagerCallbackAggregator completes the sender with
the first non-null reply (WebExtensionContextAPIRuntimeCocoa.mm).
Blink’s MessageService in the browser process opens a channel with
OpenChannelToExtension() to the extension’s frames and service worker
(OpenChannelImpl()), delivers through DispatchMessage(), and closes the
channel in CloseChannel() when the receivers’ ports go away
(message_service.cc). Gecko’s ProxyMessenger.recvRuntimeMessage() in
ExtensionParent.sys.mjs casts the message to every child context of
the extension through ConduitsParent’s castRuntimeMessage() and
resolves with the first defined response.

Runtime messages go only to an extension’s own pages, never to content
scripts, as in Blink’s ExtensionMessagePort::CreateForExtension()
and WebKit’s WebExtensionContext::runtimeSendMessage(). Gecko’s
ConduitsParent::_cast() keeps content scripts out for a content-script
sender, but still has a route from extension pages to content scripts.

The broker takes the sender from its own view of the sending document,
as Blink’s ValidateSourceContextAndExtractExtensionId() validates
the source through its RenderFrameHost and Gecko’s verifyEnv() checks
principals and ancestry. A dispatch id answers only from the page
it went to, as Gecko’s ConduitsParent::receiveMessage() rejects the
wrong actor and WebKit’s Connection::takeAsyncReplyHandler() keeps
its handlers per connection.

While a background page starts, a message waits for it, as with Blink’s
MessageService::EnqueuePendingMessageForLazyBackgroundLoad(), Gecko’s
wakeupBackground(), which normalizeArgs() awaits, and WebKit’s
m_actionsToPerformAfterBackgroundContentLoads. The 30-second deadline
for a page that never loads is Ladybird’s own.
